Files — reading and writing.

Path names a location; files is the class of static methods that actually do things to it — read it, write it, copy it, ask if it exists. This is where your program meets the disk, and where the input-output exceptions of this module's first half become concrete. The good news: for the common cases, one method call does the whole job — resource handling included.

Start with the whole-file shortcuts — the ninety-percent case. For a file that comfortably fits in memory, files gives you one-line read and write. No streams to open, no try-with-resources to write — the method opens, transfers, and closes internally. So files-dot-read-string gives you the whole file as a string. Files-dot-read-all-lines gives you the whole file as a list of strings. Files-dot-read-all-bytes gives you a byte array. Going the other way, files-dot-write-string writes a string to the file, creating or truncating it. And files-dot-write, of a list, puts each element on its own line. These are the methods you'll reach for most. And read-string and write-string, from Java eleven, default to U-T-F-eight — a deliberate, portable choice; don't rely on the platform's default charset.

You can control write behaviour with an open-option. By default, write-string and write create the file if it's absent, and truncate it if it's present — a full overwrite. To append instead, pass standard-open-option-append, and it adds to the end. To demand that the file not already exist, pass standard-open-option-create-new, and it fails if the file is there.

Now, when the file is large, stream it — don't slurp it. Read-all-lines loads the entire file into memory — that's fine for a config file, but ruinous for a ten-gigabyte log. For large or unbounded input, open a lazy, auto-closeable stream, and let it flow one line at a time. So try, open-paren, stream-of-string lines equals files-dot-lines of path, close-paren — then filter the lines that contain "ERROR" and count them. And that stream must be closed, because it holds an open file handle. This is the module coming full circle: a files-dot-lines stream is a module-seven pipeline, whose source is a file — and because it holds a file handle, it must be opened in a try-with-resources, from section six. So: slurp small files, stream large ones.

Files also manipulates the files themselves — not just their contents. Existence checks, copies, moves, deletes — and each one throws a descriptive input-output-exception on failure, rather than the old file's silent false. Files-dot-exists and files-dot-is-directory ask about a path. Files-dot-create-file makes one, and files-dot-create-directories makes a directory and its parents too. Files-dot-copy, with standard-copy-option-replace-existing, copies over an existing target. Files-dot-move moves. Files-dot-delete deletes — and throws if the file is absent. And files-dot-delete-if-exists returns false instead of throwing.

So here's the rule of thumb. Small file, whole contents — use read-string, read-all-lines, or write-string; one call, done. Large or streaming — use files-dot-lines, or a new-buffered-reader, inside a try-with-resources. And the file itself — exists, copy, move, delete; and expect an input-output-exception, so wrap it or declare it.

Underneath, everything obeys the two halves of this module at once. The operations throw checked input-output exceptions you must handle. And the streaming ones are auto-closeable resources you must close. Files are where exceptions, resources, and streams all meet.
